      "description": "The number of days in which the capture from authorization to settlement needs to happen. Possible values are '3-5' (between 3 and 5 days), '>5' (more than 5 days is possible), '<3' (before 3 days), 'immediate', or 'manual'. The faster the capture to settlement happens, the more expensive it is."
    },
    {
      "id": "c59d36c9-7927-4039-8e73-890e35ac0932",
      "name": "Fee rule null means all values",
      "description": "If a field is set to null it means that it applies to all possible values of that field. E.g. null value in aci means that the rules applies for all possible values of aci."
    },
    {
      "id": "4820eb9a-8a6f-4c7a-a6da-e6fc92c65716",
      "name": "Fee rule wildcards are empty lists, not nulls",
      "description": "In the fees table, the list fields account_type, merchant_category_code and aci are never NULL. A rule that applies to every value stores an EMPTY list. So a rule matches a value when the list is empty OR contains the value: (ARRAY_LENGTH(aci) = 0 OR 'B' IN UNNEST(aci)). The scalar fields is_credit, intracountry, capture_delay, monthly_volume and monthly_fraud_level use NULL for 'applies to all': a credit transaction matches rules with is_credit = TRUE OR is_credit IS NULL."
    },
    {
      "id": "559de6a8-0422-40b9-bf7e-b442bb9e9c39",
      "name": "How to answer 'steer traffic' and 'move transactions to another ACI' questions",
      "description": "'To which card scheme should the merchant steer traffic' means: re-price ALL of the merchant's transactions in the period as if each one had gone through that card scheme (keep every other transaction attribute), total the fees per scheme, and answer with the scheme and its total. Do not compare schemes only on the transactions each already processed. 'Move the fraudulent transactions to a different ACI' means: re-price the merchant's fraudulent transactions in the period as if each had that ACI, total per candidate ACI, and answer with the ACI (not a card scheme)."
    },
    {
      "id": "8b9bea55-0d3d-4781-9a0e-772615e13e3b",
      "name": "Intracountry (local acquiring)",
      "description": "A transaction is domestic (intracountry) when the issuer country and the acquiring country are the same. Local acquiring refers to the scenario where the issuer country is the same as the acquirer country. International transactions, where the issuer country and acquirer country are different, typically are more expensive."
